WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.039
The squire went to Bristol to outfit a ship and get us ready to go to sea, and it took longer than he thought.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:17.116
The doctor had to go to London to seek a physician to take over his practice, and I stayed at the squire’s house with Tom Redruth, his loyal gamekeeper.

3
00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:26.502
I spent all of my time brooding over the map, and I was full of sea dreams and anticipations of strange islands.

4
00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:33.479
But I never imagined anything as strange and tragic as what our actual adventures would be.

5
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.879
After I’d spent many weeks daydreaming by the fire, Tom came to me and said, "This letter arrived.

6
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:50.579
It’s addressed to Dr. Livesey but says ‘To be opened, in his absence, by Tom Redruth or young Hawkins.’ I can only read print, so you read it."

7
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.716
I opened the letter and read aloud:

8
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:58.785
Old Anchor Inn, Bristol, March 1, 1746

9
00:00:58.786 --> 00:01:00.661
Dear Livesey—

10
00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:06.071
I don’t know whether you are at home or in London, so I’m sending this letter to both

11
00:01:06.072 --> 00:01:12.069
places. The ship is ready, and you won’t believe how sweet she is—a child could sail

12
00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:19.066
her! Her name is Hispaniola, and my old friend Blandly purchased her for a trifle and worked hard to get her ready for

13
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:26.980
us. So did everyone in Bristol, as soon as they got wind of what we’d be searching for—treasure, I

14
00:01:26.981 --> 00:01:30.411
mean. "Oh, Dr. Livesey won’t like that," I

15
00:01:30.412 --> 00:01:33.404
commented. "He told the squire not to talk about the

16
00:01:33.405 --> 00:01:38.381
treasure." "He can talk if he wants—he’s the squire!" Tom

17
00:01:38.382 --> 00:01:43.135
grumbled. I gave up any more attempts at commentary and continued to

18
00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:45.755
read. Finding a crew was

19
00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:54.695
difficult. I needed about twenty men, and I had a devil of a time finding even half a dozen, but then the most remarkable luck brought me the very man I

20
00:01:54.696 --> 00:01:59.611
required. I was standing on the dock, and by accident, I started talking with

21
00:01:59.612 --> 00:02:05.317
him. He’s an old sailor who owns a tavern and knows all the seafaring men in

22
00:02:05.318 --> 00:02:10.913
Bristol. He told me he’d been ashore a long time, and wanted a position as cook to get to sea

23
00:02:10.914 --> 00:02:15.533
again. He’d hobbled down there that morning, he said, just to breathe the salt

24
00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:20.751
air. I was touched and, out of pure pity, I hired him on the spot to be the ship’s

25
00:02:20.752 --> 00:02:29.547
cook. He’s called Long John Silver and he lost a leg in the service of England—which I thought was a strong recommendation as to his character.

26
00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:34.752
He helped me find the crew, and we put together a company of the toughest old sailors imaginable—not

27
00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:39.248
pretty to look at, but they have the most indomitable spirit. Long

28
00:02:39.249 --> 00:02:48.490
John even got rid of two of the men I’d already hired, after he showed me they wouldn’t be suitable for an adventure of this importance.

29
00:02:48.491 --> 00:02:55.789
I’m eating like a bull and sleeping like a tree, yet I shall not enjoy a moment until we are under way. Seaward, ho!

30
00:02:55.790 --> 00:03:02.131
Let young Hawkins stop to see his mother, with Tom Redruth, and then both should come to Bristol immediately.

31
00:03:02.132 --> 00:03:04.686
—John Trelawney

32
00:03:04.687 --> 00:03:13.871
Postscript—Blandly also found an admirable fellow to captain the ship; he’s a bit businesslike and stiff, but in all other respects, a treasure.

33
00:03:13.872 --> 00:03:23.088
And Long John Silver unearthed a very competent first mate, a man named Arrow, so things shall go very well on board the good ship Hispaniola.

34
00:03:23.089 --> 00:03:25.282
J. T. "Hooray!"

35
00:03:25.283 --> 00:03:34.274
I shouted, gleeful to be starting our adventure, even if I did have to travel with a grumpy old man like Tom Redruth. The

36
00:03:34.275 --> 00:03:41.081
next morning, we set out on foot for the Admiral Benbow, and I found Mother in good spirits. "The

37
00:03:41.082 --> 00:03:47.766
squire arranged for repairs, some repainting, and new furniture," said Mother. "There’s

38
00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:51.249
even a beautiful new armchair for me. He

39
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:56.853
also hired a boy, so I won’t need help while you’re gone," she added. All

40
00:03:56.854 --> 00:04:01.539
I had thought of up until then had been the adventures in front of me, but

41
00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:08.643
meeting this boy who would be taking my place made me feel homesick already and a bit angry. "You

42
00:04:08.644 --> 00:04:13.925
missed a spot," I said to him, pointing out a dirty area on the table he was cleaning. The

43
00:04:13.926 --> 00:04:22.310
next day, after dinner, I said good-bye to Mother, and the cove where I’d lived since I was born, and the dear, old Admiral Benbow. Within

44
00:04:22.311 --> 00:04:29.456
minutes, Tom Redruth and I turned a corner in the road and my home was out of sight. A

45
00:04:29.457 --> 00:04:34.259
stagecoach picked us up at dusk, and I dozed the whole way. When

46
00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.051
I opened my eyes, it was morning and we were in a bustling city. "Where

47
00:04:39.052 --> 00:04:41.414
are we?" I asked.

48
00:04:41.415 --> 00:04:45.131
"Bristol," Tom said. "Get out."

49
00:04:45.132 --> 00:04:51.863
Mr. Trelawney was rooming at an inn down by the docks to supervise the work being done on the ship.

50
00:04:51.864 --> 00:05:00.387
Tom and I started walking to the inn, and on the way, we saw a great multitude of ships of all sizes and from all nations.

51
00:05:00.388 --> 00:05:09.827
On one ship, sailors were singing at their work, and on another, men were aloft, high up on ropes that seemed no thicker than a spider’s thread.

52
00:05:09.828 --> 00:05:18.916
"I can’t believe I’m going to sea, to an unknown island, on a ship with singing seamen!" I said excitedly to Tom.

53
00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:24.973
Suddenly, we came to the inn and met Squire Trelawney, who was all dressed up like an admiral.

54
00:05:24.974 --> 00:05:32.669
"Here you are," he cried. "The doctor came last night. Bravo! The ship’s company is complete!"

55
00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.332
"Oh, sir," I cried, "when do we sail?"

56
00:05:36.333 --> 00:05:45.861
"Sail?" he said with a laugh. "We sail tomorrow!"

